WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.866 --> 00:00:02.335
-Yeah! Nick, oh, my gosh.

00:00:02.335 --> 00:00:03.802
It is great to see you, buddy.

00:00:03.802 --> 00:00:05.738
Thank you so much
for being here.

00:00:05.738 --> 00:00:09.175
Where -- where are you
Zooming from right now?

00:00:09.175 --> 00:00:11.310
-Hello, Jimmy, thank you
for having me.

00:00:11.310 --> 00:00:14.314
I'm coming at you from the farm

00:00:14.314 --> 00:00:16.682
where we shoot our show,
"Making It,"

00:00:16.682 --> 00:00:18.350
the crafting competition
show...

00:00:18.350 --> 00:00:20.420
-Of course.
-...on NBC.

00:00:20.420 --> 00:00:22.388
-You're back to work,
oh, that's great.

00:00:22.388 --> 00:00:25.658
How does it feel? Is it weird?
Is it safe? Is it good?

00:00:25.658 --> 00:00:27.160
What does it feel like?

00:00:27.160 --> 00:00:30.096
-It feels like the end
of a really good movie

00:00:30.096 --> 00:00:34.401
that, like, where normal humans
would cry,

00:00:34.401 --> 00:00:37.703
and I even well up a bit.
-[ Laughing ]

00:00:37.703 --> 00:00:39.339
-No.
-It feels so good,

00:00:39.339 --> 00:00:41.174
because I've spent
all this time

00:00:41.174 --> 00:00:44.277
feeling like I wasn't of any
use to anybody.

00:00:44.277 --> 00:00:45.678
-Yeah.

00:00:45.678 --> 00:00:47.746
-So I'm a farm animal.

00:00:47.746 --> 00:00:50.416
Like, if you put me to work
in the field

00:00:50.416 --> 00:00:51.985
and then I feel good,

00:00:51.985 --> 00:00:54.454
and then I can have my carrot
and my apple

00:00:54.454 --> 00:00:57.957
at the end of the day,
by which I mean my scotch.

00:00:57.957 --> 00:00:59.725
-[ Laughs ]

00:00:59.725 --> 00:01:02.461
But this is -- so you're there
with Amy

00:01:02.461 --> 00:01:04.997
and all the contestants
and the crew and everything?

00:01:04.997 --> 00:01:06.132
Is it --

00:01:06.132 --> 00:01:08.401
-Somebody way smarter than me,

00:01:08.401 --> 00:01:12.204
one of our producers,
I guess they consulted

00:01:12.204 --> 00:01:15.808
the science or, like,
what the doctors are saying.

00:01:15.808 --> 00:01:20.680
And so, they've put everybody
in masks and face shields,

00:01:20.680 --> 00:01:23.916
and they've, what do they --
they call them protocols.

00:01:23.916 --> 00:01:26.286
We adhere to these protocols.

00:01:26.286 --> 00:01:28.254
-Yeah.
-Social distancing.

00:01:28.254 --> 00:01:32.092
All these newfangled,
like, trends.

00:01:32.092 --> 00:01:33.626
-And they work.
-And so you look around,

00:01:33.626 --> 00:01:36.362
we're shooting this show
and everybody's is in a mask,

00:01:36.362 --> 00:01:38.898
and they're complying
with these, like,

00:01:38.898 --> 00:01:41.401
"science ideas."
-Wow.

00:01:41.401 --> 00:01:45.671
-And we're able to produce
our show successfully.

00:01:45.671 --> 00:01:49.742
No one is catching
the coronavirus.

00:01:49.742 --> 00:01:51.511
-Wow.
-So, I wish that --

00:01:51.511 --> 00:01:53.712
I mean, I don't know how
they figured this out,

00:01:53.712 --> 00:01:57.850
but I wish there was a way to
communicate this to, like,

00:01:57.850 --> 00:02:00.420
the country or, like,
our government or something.

00:02:00.420 --> 00:02:02.755
Because I feel like
if they found out about it,

00:02:02.755 --> 00:02:04.891
they would tell everybody,
and they'd be like,

00:02:04.891 --> 00:02:08.761
"Hey, everybody, if you follow
these, like, science ideas,

00:02:08.761 --> 00:02:11.830
you can go to work and be safe
and not die,

00:02:11.830 --> 00:02:13.432
all that [bleep]."
-Yeah.

00:02:13.432 --> 00:02:15.101
-Here's the thing,
when I look around and see

00:02:15.101 --> 00:02:19.638
all of us being safe,
the idea that hasn't been

00:02:19.638 --> 00:02:21.741
fully disseminated is,

00:02:21.741 --> 00:02:23.542
when you wear
the protective equipment,

00:02:23.542 --> 00:02:25.512
it's not to protect yourself,

00:02:25.512 --> 00:02:27.947
it's to protect the others
around you

00:02:27.947 --> 00:02:30.482
in case you have it,
which you might.

00:02:30.482 --> 00:02:34.019
And so, when I see everybody
doing this,

00:02:34.019 --> 00:02:36.923
I take it as a sign
of affection.

00:02:36.923 --> 00:02:40.059
It's me and my neighbors
and my co-workers,

00:02:40.059 --> 00:02:43.396
and no one asks what our
political beliefs are.

00:02:43.396 --> 00:02:47.000
We just see each other working
together and say, "I love you,

00:02:47.000 --> 00:02:48.834
and thank you for loving me.

00:02:48.834 --> 00:02:51.070
We can do our job.
We can earn our money.

00:02:51.070 --> 00:02:53.640
We can feed our families."
-That's right.

00:02:53.640 --> 00:02:58.877
-In my case, my wife and I can
use our Japanese sex swing.

00:02:58.877 --> 00:03:01.981
We can afford meals.
Keep our energy up.

00:03:01.981 --> 00:03:04.350
Have marathon sessions.
-[ Laughs ]

00:03:04.350 --> 00:03:06.186
-We can buy Vaseline
when we get chafed.

00:03:06.186 --> 00:03:07.954
-Alright, yeah, yeah,
we get the point.

00:03:07.954 --> 00:03:09.589
Yeah, yeah, yeah.
Absolutely.

00:03:09.589 --> 00:03:13.225
Nick Offerman,
you found a way to help

00:03:13.225 --> 00:03:16.395
and entertain people
at the same time.

00:03:16.395 --> 00:03:19.431
Because you've made all of
your comedy specials available

00:03:19.431 --> 00:03:25.137
now on your website, with the
proceeds going to charity.

00:03:25.137 --> 00:03:27.239
I think that's an awesome move.

00:03:27.239 --> 00:03:30.876
And was it the quarantine
that inspired you to do this,

00:03:30.876 --> 00:03:32.711
or what...

00:03:32.711 --> 00:03:35.514
-No, it's sort of --

00:03:35.514 --> 00:03:38.283
it's not anything that I should
be proud of.

00:03:38.283 --> 00:03:41.587
Like, I've been touring as
a humorist for some years,

00:03:41.587 --> 00:03:46.559
so I have -- this audiobook
is my fourth special.

00:03:46.559 --> 00:03:51.163
My first three specials --
I'm terrible at promoting them,

00:03:51.163 --> 00:03:53.599
and so, it took me a long time,

00:03:53.599 --> 00:03:58.204
and seven's and 10's of people
were asking me,

00:03:58.204 --> 00:04:00.472
"Where can I see your specials?"

00:04:00.472 --> 00:04:03.509
So I finally succeeded,
after literally years,

00:04:03.509 --> 00:04:06.778
of getting them on a website
that people can see,

00:04:06.778 --> 00:04:11.617
but I'm so late that some --
some nefarious criminal

00:04:11.617 --> 00:04:14.653
has already secured
nickofferman.com.

00:04:14.653 --> 00:04:18.858
So, I -- I have acquired

00:04:18.858 --> 00:04:21.527
nickofferman.co.

00:04:21.527 --> 00:04:24.197
So one letter short.
-So nickofferman.co.

00:04:24.197 --> 00:04:27.233
-Nickofferman.co.
-So no "M."

00:04:27.233 --> 00:04:29.802
-I couldn't not afford the "M."
-Understood.

00:04:29.802 --> 00:04:32.171
-The thing is, the timing
was such that

00:04:32.171 --> 00:04:35.341
right when the website
was ready to launch,

00:04:35.341 --> 00:04:39.278
the pandemic hit,
and everybody was in trouble.

00:04:39.278 --> 00:04:43.016
-Yeah.
-People are out of work,

00:04:43.016 --> 00:04:46.385
you know, the whole country,
the whole world is suffering.

00:04:46.385 --> 00:04:49.855
And I said, "Well, jeez,
now what do I do?

00:04:49.855 --> 00:04:52.592
It's not great timing to be
like, you guys,

00:04:52.592 --> 00:04:56.295
I'm sorry about everybody
in the hospital and stuff,

00:04:56.295 --> 00:05:00.867
but check out my crazy
jackass comedy specials."

00:05:00.867 --> 00:05:03.135
And so I just thought,
you know what?

00:05:03.135 --> 00:05:06.038
At least 37 people have said
they wanted to see this.

00:05:06.038 --> 00:05:08.941
So, for those people,
I'm going to put this up.

00:05:08.941 --> 00:05:12.845
But all the proceeds -- it costs
a few bucks to see my special,

00:05:12.845 --> 00:05:17.283
and all the profits go to
America's Food Fund.

00:05:17.283 --> 00:05:19.318
So, I was like, okay...
-[ Clapping ]

00:05:19.318 --> 00:05:22.888
-Get these 37 people
with questionable taste,

00:05:22.888 --> 00:05:24.623
I will take their money.
-I love that.

00:05:24.623 --> 00:05:28.227
-And we will buy sandwiches
for at least 11 people.

00:05:28.227 --> 00:05:29.962
-That's awesome that
you did that, buddy.

00:05:29.962 --> 00:05:31.497
I think that's cool.

00:05:31.497 --> 00:05:35.467
I want to talk about
"All Rise."

00:05:35.467 --> 00:05:39.905
This is -- first of all, who did
the drawing and the cartoon?

00:05:39.905 --> 00:05:42.007
-My friend Matt Davies.

00:05:42.007 --> 00:05:45.445
He's a Pulitzer Prize-winning
political cartoonist

00:05:45.445 --> 00:05:47.546
who works for the
publication "Newsday."

00:05:47.546 --> 00:05:49.114
-Yeah.
-So funny.

00:05:49.114 --> 00:05:51.617
-This was the live show that
you were going to tour with?

00:05:51.617 --> 00:05:53.352
-It is. Yeah.

00:05:53.352 --> 00:05:57.522
And I toured a bunch of America
with it last year,

00:05:57.522 --> 00:05:59.858
and also Australia
and New Zealand,

00:05:59.858 --> 00:06:01.961
and my cool publisher
called Dutton...

00:06:01.961 --> 00:06:03.196
-Okay.

00:06:03.196 --> 00:06:06.131
...under the auspices of
Penguin Random House.

00:06:06.131 --> 00:06:08.101
They came and saw me
at The Beacon

00:06:08.101 --> 00:06:10.436
on the Upper West side
and they said,

00:06:10.436 --> 00:06:13.339
"Hey, what if we turn this into
like an audiobook original."

00:06:13.339 --> 00:06:16.242
So, it's like a humor show.

00:06:16.242 --> 00:06:21.881
It's like me reading my essays
and performing my dumb songs.

00:06:21.881 --> 00:06:24.450
One of them is very poignant
right now,

00:06:24.450 --> 00:06:26.752
about Brett Kavanaugh,
called "I Like Beer."

00:06:26.752 --> 00:06:28.020
-Oh.

00:06:28.020 --> 00:06:33.292
-And, the, you know,
the Supreme Court justices

00:06:33.292 --> 00:06:36.295
and their legitimacy
or lack thereof

00:06:36.295 --> 00:06:39.064
has been in the news recently.
-Sure.

00:06:39.064 --> 00:06:40.666
-So, you know,
I thought it was --

00:06:40.666 --> 00:06:43.102
it's a great opportunity
to celebrate that guy

00:06:43.102 --> 00:06:44.537
and how much he loves beer.

00:06:44.537 --> 00:06:46.538
-Here's my one question
for Nick Offerman.

00:06:46.538 --> 00:06:49.374
If we listen to this,

00:06:49.374 --> 00:06:52.411
what are we missing
of seeing you live?

00:06:52.411 --> 00:06:57.216
Like, what would you -- are we
missing out on anything?

00:06:57.216 --> 00:06:58.751
-That's a great question,
Jimmy.

00:06:58.751 --> 00:07:02.421
I mean, obviously,
you're going to get

00:07:02.421 --> 00:07:06.025
the soothing sounds
of like Brian Eno,

00:07:06.025 --> 00:07:07.893
"Music For Airport,"

00:07:07.893 --> 00:07:10.595
if they were a hirsute baritone

00:07:10.595 --> 00:07:12.765
coming into your ears,

00:07:12.765 --> 00:07:15.167
and that will put you to sleep,
which is great.

00:07:15.167 --> 00:07:17.102
We all need to get some rest.

00:07:17.102 --> 00:07:18.571
-Mm-hmm.
-That's wonderful.

00:07:18.571 --> 00:07:21.573
But, what you are missing is
the rest of your senses.

00:07:21.573 --> 00:07:23.308
Think about it.
-That's true.

00:07:23.308 --> 00:07:25.644
-You're not going to see me
dance and, like,

00:07:25.644 --> 00:07:29.915
just subtly move my hips in that
way that drives everybody crazy.

00:07:29.915 --> 00:07:33.786
You know what I'm talking about.
But also, there are some --

00:07:33.786 --> 00:07:36.722
there are some stopgap measures
you can pursue.

00:07:36.722 --> 00:07:38.658
Like, if you are going to
listen to this,

00:07:38.658 --> 00:07:42.061
get yourself a nice tray
of charcuterie,

00:07:42.061 --> 00:07:46.565
maybe some sliced up --
some smoked applewood cheddar,

00:07:46.565 --> 00:07:50.836
and then have
a single malt scotch

00:07:50.836 --> 00:07:54.340
from the island of Islay,
and just waft those things

00:07:54.340 --> 00:07:55.841
under your nose.
-All together.

00:07:55.841 --> 00:07:58.844
And if you -- if you
live in an area --

00:07:58.844 --> 00:08:02.014
most of the upper Midwest,
where the soil is

00:08:02.014 --> 00:08:04.116
particularly loamy,

00:08:04.116 --> 00:08:06.852
get a bowl of that soil,
throw it in the microwave

00:08:06.852 --> 00:08:08.654
for about 90 seconds,

00:08:08.654 --> 00:08:12.024
and then just these aromas
will help recreate

00:08:12.024 --> 00:08:17.095
the fecund musk that I tend to
fill a theater with

00:08:17.095 --> 00:08:19.999
when I'm dancing
about the stage.

00:08:19.999 --> 00:08:22.034
-That is perfect.
I will do that this weekend.

00:08:22.034 --> 00:08:25.338
-If you dig fertility, you're
gonna love my scent.

00:08:25.338 --> 00:08:27.606
[ Laughter ]

00:08:27.606 --> 00:08:30.175
-Everyone go listen
and experience

00:08:30.175 --> 00:08:32.645
Nick's audiobook
"All Rise."

00:08:32.645 --> 00:08:35.214
Nick Offerman, thank you
so much for being here.

00:08:35.214 --> 00:08:38.217
Be safe, say hi to your wife,
please, and give her my love

00:08:38.217 --> 00:08:39.685
and say hi to Amy too, please.

00:08:39.685 --> 00:08:42.054
-I will. Thank you, Jimmy.
It's great to see you.

00:08:42.054 --> 00:08:43.889
And you know what, can I say one
more thing real quick?

00:08:43.889 --> 00:08:45.357
-Please, yep.

00:08:45.357 --> 00:08:47.493
-I feel like we're
a few days away

00:08:47.493 --> 00:08:49.428
from a presidential election,

00:08:49.428 --> 00:08:51.897
in case the audience
hasn't heard,

00:08:51.897 --> 00:08:53.599
and the only people --

00:08:53.599 --> 00:08:55.534
I think most people are
probably pretty decided.

00:08:55.534 --> 00:08:58.303
But when I was young
and I was ambivalent

00:08:58.303 --> 00:09:02.708
and was, like, too cool
to worry about what was going on

00:09:02.708 --> 00:09:04.443
with who I would vote for,

00:09:04.443 --> 00:09:06.812
if there's anybody
like that listening,

00:09:06.812 --> 00:09:10.048
I just want to make you think
for one second.

00:09:10.048 --> 00:09:12.018
What could be more punk?

00:09:12.018 --> 00:09:15.320
If you're, like, cynical and
"who cares who gets elected?"

00:09:15.320 --> 00:09:18.157
What could be more punk than
going out and voting?

00:09:18.157 --> 00:09:20.326
-I love that.
Nick Offerman, everybody.

00:09:20.326 --> 00:09:21.694
"All Rise" is available now.

